theory.intro:
For a normally distributed variable, the **empirical rule** gives the percentage of data lying within a whole number of standard deviations of the mean. About $68\%$ falls within $1$ standard deviation, $95\%$ within $2$, and $99.7\%$ within $3$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.4cm, y=1cm]
\draw[thick] plot[domain=-3.7:3.7, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[->] (-4.1,0) -- (4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw (-3,0) -- (-3,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu-3\sigma$};
\draw (-2,0) -- (-2,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu-2\sigma$};
\draw (-1,0) -- (-1,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu-\sigma$};
\draw (0,0) -- (0,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu$};
\draw (1,0) -- (1,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu+\sigma$};
\draw (2,0) -- (2,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu+2\sigma$};
\draw (3,0) -- (3,-0.12) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\mu+3\sigma$};
\draw[<->] (-1,1.75) -- (1,1.75);
\node[font=\small] at (0,2.05) {$68\%$};
\draw[<->] (-2,2.75) -- (2,2.75);
\node[font=\small] at (0,3.05) {$95\%$};
\draw[<->] (-3,3.55) -- (3,3.55);
\node[font=\small] at (0,3.85) {$99.7\%$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

